{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/full-professor-banking-finance": {
      "title": "Full Professor Banking & Finance",
      "description": "Full Professor Banking & Finance - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs Full Professor Banking & Finance Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Close submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Close submenu + Remuneration, insurance, reimbursement Work-life balance Sports and culture Professional development Close submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests Full Professor Banking & Finance Faculty: Faculty of Law, Economics and Governance Department: Utrecht University School of Economics Hours per week: 28 to 40 Application deadline: 1 November 2026 Utrecht University School of Economics (U.S.E.) is a young and growing Economics department. The position of the Chair in Banking and Finance contributes to the (inter)national reputation and profile of U.S.E. in line with its multidisciplinary vision on research, teaching, and impact in the area of banking and finance. The Chair plays a crucial role in strengthening the international research profile and expertise of U.S.E. and its Finance section. Your job The main goal of the Chair in Banking and Finance is to further advance research (aligned with the relevant UU strategic themes, mainly “ Institutions for Open Societies ” and/or “ Pathways to Sustainability ”), and education in the relevant fields of expertise. Apart from their own research, we expect the Chair to play a stimulating role in the USE research community; to promote a research environment in line with USE’s aims for Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion; and to attract external research funding through grants and/or consulting. Additional duties include supervision of PhD Candidates and mentoring of (Post-Doctoral) researchers resulting in state-of-the-art PhD-manuscripts and publications, and the transfer of knowledge to society by organizing events (such as conferences and workshops). Teaching of the chair will connect the field with other relevant disciplines, in line with U.S.E.’s Real World Perspective. Cooperation with other USE sections and other departments and faculties within UU in teaching and research is strongly encouraged. The tasks and responsibilities of the Chair will include: Performing and encouraging scientific research in the field of Banking and Finance; Supervision of PhD students and post-Doctoral researchers; Participating in education in the U.S.E. Bachelor and Master programs, as well as executive education; Leadership in contributing to the research and teaching climate, including stimulating and supervising multidisciplinary research and teaching; Contributing to the financial sustainability of U.S.E., including active participation in external fundraising. Your qualities The person we are looking for is a passionate and creative academic with a strong background in finance and a genuine interest in Banking and Finance. Given the current developments in this field, knowledge/research on digital/data science and AI, and the impact upon Finance – including  its role for enabling societal transitions - is highly valued. We expect the chair to have a vision on the impact of data and societal transitions on finance and the financial industry. The person should be able to integrate digital knowledge in courses in the academic Finance master programs. The ideal candidate for this position has a strong publication record and teaching experience in the field of Banking and/or Finance. The candidate is also willing and able to reach out to researchers active in other fields, such as economics and management. This requires an open and inquisitive mind, and a willingness to experiment and explore new avenues and methods in research and academic teaching. We anticipate that the candidate is willing and able to communicate complex scientific results and insights to an audience of policy makers, societal stakeholders, and the wider public; proven ability in this respect is considered a plus. The following profile will be expected of the chair holder: A doctorate in the field of finance or economics; A strong track record in research, as demonstrated among others by publications in internationally recognised journals, a high number of citations, and preferably membership of policy advisor bodies (e.g. CEPR, NBER, etc); Impact in the field, as demonstrated by e.g. editiorial roles, conference organisation, invited talks or keynote lectures, engagement with policy or professional organisations, or other forms of societal or academic impact; An interest in and commitment to multidisciplinary research and collaboration across disciplinary boundaries; Significant experience in academic leadership, demonstrated, for example, through leadership roles, the development of teams or research programmes, mentoring and supporting colleagues, or other contributions to an inclusive, socially safe and collaborative academic environment, together with a clear vision on academic leadership; Enthusiasm and commitment to the further development of research and teaching in banking and/or Finance from a multidisciplinary perspective within UU; Ability to acquire external funding; Good communication skills; Fluency in English; ability to communicate in Dutch is preferred. Our offer an employment contract for 12 months with the prospect of a permanent employment contract; a working week of 28- 40 hours and a gross monthly salary between € 7.475 and €10.815 in the case of full-time employment (salary scale H2 under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)); 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment laid down in the CAO NU, Utrecht University also offers a range of its own schemes for employees. This includes arrangements for professional development , various types of leave, and options for sports and cultural activities . You can also tailor your employment conditions through our Terms of Employment Options Model. In this way, we encourage you to keep investing in your personal and professional development. For more information, please visit Working at Utrecht University . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . The Faculty of Law, Economics and Governance is a faculty at the heart of society, with a strong focus on social issues. Our subjects of Law, Economics and Governance give us a strong mix of academic disciplines that complement and enhance each other. This is a unique combination and a mark of our faculty's strength. The Utrecht University School of Economics (U.S.E.) is part of the Faculty of Law, Economics and Governance. U.S.E. aims to contribute to an economy where people flourish by taking a broad view on welfare and its causes. We aspire to be an internationally renowned school of economics with scientifically robust and socially relevant research and education. We enrich economics with other disciplines to better solve problems and identify opportunities, from a business as well as government perspective. U.S.E.’s Bachelor’s programme Economics & Business Economics offers students a solid foundation in economic theory and methods, combined with insights from business economics, finance, entrepreneurship, innovation, ethics and sustainability. The programme has about 1,500 students from various backgrounds and nationalities and is completely taught in English. More information For more information about this position, please contact prof. dr. Niels Bosma, Head of Department Utrecht University School of Economics, at n.s.bosma@uu.nl , or prof. dr. Frank Verbeeten, Head of Section Finance Utrecht University School of Economics, at f.h.m.verbeeten@uu.nl . The structural report for this vacancy contains more information on Utrecht School of Economics and can be requested from Babette Berrocal, b.p.m.berrocalvargas-vandenbrink@uu.nl . First interviews are planned to take place in December 2026 Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion . If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Adam Smith Hall , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page . A valid Certificate of Conduct (VOG) is required for this position. This will be requested as part of the application process. Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. To apply, please send your curriculum vitae, including a letter of motivation, via the ‘apply now’ button. The application deadline is 1 November 2026. Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/postdoc-ai-driven-urban-redesign-for-health": {
      "title": "Postdoc: AI-driven Urban (Re)Design for Health",
      "description": "Postdoc: AI-driven Urban (Re)Design for Health - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs Postdoc: AI-driven Urban (Re)Design for Health Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Close submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Close submenu + Remuneration, insurance, reimbursement Work-life balance Sports and culture Professional development Close submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests Postdoc: AI-driven Urban (Re)Design for Health Faculty: Faculty of Geosciences Department: Department of Human Geography and Spatial Planning Hours per week: 36 to 40 Application deadline: 15 October 2026 Do you want to use artificial intelligence, geospatial data science, and urban analytics to create healthier and more equitable cities? Are you excited about developing innovative AI systems that translate scientific evidence into practical urban design solutions? Your job At Utrecht University, you will be part of the transdisciplinary ELEVATE project: Healthy living environments create healthy physical activity-related habits. As a postdoctoral researcher, you will lead the development of an innovative AI-enabled recommender system for urban (re)design. The system will combine street-view imagery (SVIs), geospatial data, mobility data, and qualitative insights to generate evidence-based recommendations for policymakers, planners, and researchers to create healthier urban environments. You will work closely with a transdisciplinary team, including social and industrial partners. Your main responsibilities include: developing an AI-powered recommender system for healthy urban (re)design; integrating multi-source datasets, including street-view imagery, GIS data, smartphone mobility data, and qualitative insights; applying and adapting state-of-the-art foundational AI and machine learning models (e.g., multimodal AI, large language/world models) with specific finetuning for ELEVATE; designing geographically context-sensitive urban design recommendations that promote active mobility (e.g., walking, cycling) related physical activity; finetuning, evaluating, and improving model performance using local data and ensuring explainability, fairness, and robustness of the model outputs; collaborating with municipalities, urban planners, designers, and residents in model co-creation and validation workshops; contributing to the development of an interactive, publicly accessible web interface (e.g., with a chat facility) for the recommender system; publishing high-quality scientific articles in international journals; presenting findings at conferences and stakeholder events; contributing to the supervision of MSc students and collaborating within the broader ELEVATE consortium. Your qualities We are looking for an enthusiastic and collaborative researcher with a strong technical background and an interest in societal impact. You have: Must have criteria: a PhD in computer science, artificial intelligence, data science, computational social science, urban analytics, geoinformatics, or a related field; experience with foundational AI model development/fine-tuning and machine learning and/or deep learning; strong programming skills (e.g., Python, JavaScript, PostgreSQL) with clear expertise in front-end and back-end development strong communication and academic writing skills; Good to have criteria: experience working with geospatial or image-based data; an interest in urban health, mobility, public space, or sustainable cities; the ability to work independently as well as collaboratively in interdisciplinary teams. Our offer We offer: a position 1.0 FTE for 12 months. After a positive evaluation, the position can be extended to 29.5 months; a working week of 36 - 40 hours and a gross monthly salary between € 3.706 and €5.760 in the case of full-time employment (salary scale 10 under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)); 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment laid down in the CAO NU, Utrecht University also offers a range of its own schemes for employees. This includes arrangements for professional development , various types of leave, and options for sports and cultural activities . You can also tailor your employment conditions through our Terms of Employment Options Model. In this way, we encourage you to keep investing in your personal and professional development. For more information, please visit Working at Utrecht University . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . Utrecht University’s Faculty of Geosciences studies the Earth: from the Earth’s core to its surface, including man’s spatial and material utilisation of the Earth – always with a focus on sustainability and innovation. With 3,400 students (BSc and MSc) and 720 staff, the faculty is a strong and challenging organisation. The Faculty of Geosciences is organised in four Departments: Earth Sciences, Human Geography & Spatial Planning, Physical Geography, and Sustainable Development. More information For more information, please contact Marco Helbich (PI) at m.helbich@uu.nl or SM Labib at s.m.labib@uu.nl Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Vening Meineszgebouw A , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page . Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. To apply, please send, via the ‘apply now’ button: a motivation letter (max. 2 pages); your curriculum vitae (with links to published code and papers); contact details of two referees; one representative publication or project example. The application deadline is 15 October 2026. Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/phd-co-production-of-land-use-futures-for-brazil": {
      "title": "PhD: Co-production of land use futures for Brazil",
      "description": "PhD: Co-production of land use futures for Brazil - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs PhD: Co-production of land use futures for Brazil Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Open submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Open submenu + Remuneration, insurance, reimbursement Work-life balance Sports and culture Professional development Open submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests PhD: Co-production of land use futures for Brazil Faculty: Faculty of Geosciences Department: Department of Human Geography and Spatial Planning Hours per week: 36 to 40 Application deadline: 13 October 2026 Apply now Do you care about impacts of land use on the livelihoods of various groups of people in ecologically sensitive regions of the world? Do you want to explore what kind of land use futures the people aspire? And are you eager to bring these perspectives together to co-produce promising pathways towards sustainable land use futures? Then this PhD position at the Department of Human Geography and Spatial Planning of Utrecht University may be for you! Your job The MATOPIBA region in the Brazilian Cerrado is of major national and global importance: it is one of South America’s most agriculturally productive landscapes, a biodiversity hotspot, and home to Indigenous people, Quilombola, smallholder farmers, and large agribusinesses among others. Therefore, future land use strategies in MATOPIBA require balancing agricultural production with biodiversity conservation and social justice, while also accounting for the effects of climate change. In this position, you will contribute to co-producing such land use strategies by interacting with the different stakeholder groups, including policy makers, to understand how agricultural production and biodiversity impact various people’s livelihoods. Methodologically, you will use qualitative methods such as field observations, focus group discussions, semi-structured interviews, and participatory/ co-creation of maps. More specifically, you will: Travel to Brazil to conduct field research through which you interact with various actor groups and stakeholders such as policy makers and private sectors in land use in MATOPIBA; Discover their preferences in terms of ‘productive agriculture’ and ‘healthy biodiversity’ through focus groups and semi-structured interviews; Facilitate exercises of participatory mapping to co-create maps to identify desired land use futures for various groups; Further discuss and document the potential of alternative land use futures with the stakeholders in public, private, and civil society sectors; and Write academic articles and policy documents that summarize the above to generate and disseminate knowledge on the possible pathways to sustainable land use futures in MATOPIBA. This PhD position is part of the project “Frontiers of MATOPIBA landscapes”, which examines how agricultural production and biodiversity conservation can be balanced in Brazil’s MATOPIBA region under future climate and socio-economic conditions. It has the aim to support evidence-based decisions to form sustainable, just, climate-resilient land use strategies that protect biodiversity, food security, and local livelihoods. The project is fully funded through the Dutch Research Agenda and the Brazilian research council CNPq . Within the project, you will closely collaborate with another PhD candidate at Utrecht University on spatial optimization of land use, as well as with several consortium members in Brazil, and the International Institute of Social Studies (ISS) at the Erasmus University Rotterdam. Your qualities We are looking for candidates with: An MSc degree in (or nearing completion of) an MSc program in Human Geography, International Development Studies, Critical Agrarian Studies, or a related Social Science discipline; An interest in the impacts of land use on the environment and the livelihoods of people; Experience in qualitative field research and working with diverse groups of people and stakeholders; Proficiency in Portuguese; Proficiency in English; and The ability to work as part of an interdisciplinary research team. The following qualifications are desirable but not required: Experience with participatory GIS and mapping techniques; or Experience with working with rural communities, Indigenous peoples and/or Quilombola. Our offer We offer: a position (1.0 FTE) for 1 year, with an extension to a total of four years upon a successful assessment in the first year, and with the specific intent that it results in a doctorate within this period; a working week of 36 - 40 hours and a gross monthly salary between € 3.204 and €4.051 in the case of full-time employment (salary scale P under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)); 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment laid down in the CAO NU, Utrecht University also offers a range of its own schemes for employees. This includes arrangements for professional development , various types of leave, and options for sports and cultural activities . You can also tailor your employment conditions through our Terms of Employment Options Model. In this way, we encourage you to keep investing in your personal and professional development. For more information, please visit Working at Utrecht University . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . Utrecht University’s Faculty of Geosciences studies the Earth: from the Earth’s core to its surface, including man’s spatial and material utilisation of the Earth – always with a focus on sustainability and innovation. With 3,400 students (BSc and MSc) and 720 staff, the faculty is a strong and challenging organisation. The Faculty of Geosciences is organised in four Departments: Earth Sciences, Human Geography & Spatial Planning, Physical Geography, and Sustainable Development. More information For more information about this position, please contact Kei Otsuki external link at K.Otsuki@uu.nl external link Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. external link If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Vening Meineszgebouw A external link , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page external link . Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. To apply, please send your curriculum vitae, including a letter of motivation, via the ‘apply now’ button. The interviews for this position will take place on October 26th. The aimed starting date is January 1st 2027. The application deadline is 14 October 2026. Apply now Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/phd-spatial-optimization-of-land-use-in-brazil": {
      "title": "PhD: Spatial Optimization of Land Use in Brazil",
      "description": "PhD: Spatial Optimization of Land Use in Brazil - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs PhD: Spatial Optimization of Land Use in Brazil Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Open submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Open submenu + Remuneration, insurance, reimbursement Work-life balance Sports and culture Professional development Open submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests PhD: Spatial Optimization of Land Use in Brazil Faculty: Faculty of Geosciences Department: Department of Human Geography and Spatial Planning Hours per week: 36 to 40 Application deadline: 13 October 2026 Apply now Do you care about the impacts of land use in ecologically sensitive regions of the world? Are motivated to explore how rearranging land use can minimize negative impacts and maximize the positive ones for different stakeholder groups, as a basis for policy making? Are you interested in spatial optimization algorithms and uncertainty assessment? Then this PhD position at the Department of Human Geography and Spatial Planning of Utrecht University may be for you! Your job The MATOPIBA region in the Brazilian Cerrado is of major national and global importance: it is one of South America’s most agriculturally productive landscapes, a biodiversity hotspot, and home to Indigenous people, Quilombola, smallholder farmers, and large agribusinesses among others. Therefore, future land use strategies in MATOPIBA require balancing agricultural production with biodiversity conservation and social justice, while also accounting for effects of climate change. In this position, you will contribute to such land use strategies by quantifying the synergies and trade-offs between agricultural production and biodiversity conservation for different land use configurations and stakeholder preferences. Hereto, you will compute Pareto frontiers with spatial optimization algorithms. More specifically, you will: Travel to Brazil to interact with various stakeholders in land use in MATOPIBA; Translate stakeholders’ preferences in terms of ‘productive agriculture’ and ‘healthy biodiversity’ into quantifiable impact indicators; Develop a spatial optimization algorithm that finds land use configurations that optimize these indicators; Compute Pareto frontiers under different climate and socio-economic scenarios to evaluation the robustness of land use solutions for all stakeholder groups; and Write academic articles and policy documents that summarize the above to generate and disseminate knowledge on the possible pathways to sustainable land use futures in MATOPIBA. This PhD position is part of the project “Frontiers of MATOPIBA landscapes”, which examines how agricultural production and biodiversity conservation can be balanced in Brazil’s MATOPIBA region under future climate and socio-economic conditions. It has the aim to support evidence-based decisions to form sustainable, just, climate-resilient land use strategies that protect biodiversity, food security, and local livelihoods. The project is fully funded through the Dutch Research Agenda and the Brazilian research council CNPq . Within the project, you will closely collaborate with another PhD candidate at Utrecht University on stakeholder perspectives, several consortium members in Brazil, and the Department of Sustainable Development at Utrecht University. Your qualities We are looking for candidates with: An MSc degree in (or nearing completion of) an MSc program in Geoinformatics, Computer Science, (Quantitative) Geography, (Spatial / Geographic) Data Science, Environmental Science, or a related discipline; An interest in the impacts of land use; Experience in handling spatial data; Programming experience in Python, and/or R; Proficiency in English; and The ability to work as part of an interdisciplinary research team. The following qualifications are desirable but not required: Experience with (spatial) optimization; Experience with land use (change) impact assessment; or Proficiency in Portuguese. Our offer We offer: a position (1.0 FTE) for 1 year, with an extension to a total of four years upon a successful assessment in the first year, and with the specific intent that it results in a doctorate within this period; a working week of 36 - 40 hours and a gross monthly salary between € 3.204 and €4.051 in the case of full-time employment (salary scale P under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)); 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment laid down in the CAO NU, Utrecht University also offers a range of its own schemes for employees. This includes arrangements for professional development , various types of leave, and options for sports and cultural activities . You can also tailor your employment conditions through our Terms of Employment Options Model. In this way, we encourage you to keep investing in your personal and professional development. For more information, please visit Working at Utrecht University . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . Utrecht University’s Faculty of Geosciences studies the Earth: from the Earth’s core to its surface, including man’s spatial and material utilisation of the Earth – always with a focus on sustainability and innovation. With 3,400 students (BSc and MSc) and 720 staff, the faculty is a strong and challenging organisation. The Faculty of Geosciences is organised in four Departments: Earth Sciences, Human Geography & Spatial Planning, Physical Geography, and Sustainable Development. More information For more information about this position, please contact Judith Verstegen external link at j.a.verstegen@uu.nl external link Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. external link If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Vening Meineszgebouw A external link , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page external link . Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. To apply, please send your curriculum vitae, including a letter of motivation, via the ‘apply now’ button. The first round of interviews for this position will take place on October 27th. The aimed starting date is January 1st 2027. The application deadline is 14 October 2026. Apply now Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/postdoc-position-in-ai-foundation-models-for-crop-microbiomes": {
      "title": "Postdoc Position in AI Foundation Models for Crop Microbiomes",
      "description": "Postdoc Position in AI Foundation Models for Crop Microbiomes - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs Postdoc Position in AI Foundation Models for Crop Microbiomes Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Close submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Close submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests Postdoc Position in AI Foundation Models for Crop Microbiomes Faculty: Faculty of Science Department: Department of Information and Computing Sciences Hours per week: 36 to 40 Application deadline: 16 October 2026 Do you want to develop a foundation model for one of biology’s most complex ecosystems? By joining the European Innovation Council (EIC) Pathfinder project NOAH, you will design, train and implement ARCA: an AI foundation model for crop microbiomes. You will work at the interface of deep learning, bioinformatics and microbial ecology, using large-scale microbiome and genome data to learn contextual representations of microbes and communities and translate them into predictive models for successful crop microbiome engineering. Your job Plant-associated microbiomes can strongly influence crop growth, nutrition and resilience, but their behaviour depends on the crop, soil, environment and the surrounding microbial community. NOAH aims to make these context-dependent interactions learnable and predictable. At the centre of the project is ARCA (AI-guided Root microbiome engineering for ClimAte-resilient and nutritious crops), a crop microbiome foundation model trained on large-scale public and newly generated datasets. As Postdoctoral Researcher in AI, you will take a leading technical role in developing ARCA. You will explore which model architectures and learning objectives work best for sparse, high-dimensional and heterogeneous microbiome data, and turn the selected approaches into robust trainable models. Your work will cover both foundation-model pretraining and downstream predictive and generative applications. Your main responsibilities are to: • design, implement and benchmark foundation-model architectures for microbiome data, including transformer-based and masked-autoencoder approaches and relevant architectures adapted from related biological domains; • develop representations that integrate microbial identity and abundance with genomic or functional information and contextual metadata such as crop genotype, soil and environmental conditions; • define and evaluate self-supervised learning objectives and embedding strategies, and benchmark their added value against simpler machine-learning baselines; • train and evaluate ARCA on large-scale microbiome datasets, with attention to sparsity, batch effects, scalability, generalisation across studies and uncertainty in downstream predictions; • fine-tune ARCA for tasks including microbial root competence and crop-relevant outcomes, and iteratively improve the model using experimental Design-Build-Test-Learn data generated by NOAH partners; • develop a generative ARCA component, exploring autoencoder- and/or diffusion-based approaches for generating ecologically plausible microbiome configurations; • apply interpretable and explainable AI approaches to identify microbial taxa, functions and contextual features driving model predictions; • develop reproducible training and evaluation workflows and work with project partners to make models and associated tools usable beyond the immediate research setting. You will not work on an isolated AI benchmark. ARCA predictions will be tested experimentally in greenhouse and field settings and the resulting microbiome and crop phenotype data will feed back into model development. This gives you the opportunity to develop new AI methodology while seeing how model predictions perform in a real biological and agricultural system. In this position you will part of an interdisciplinary research environment spanning the AI Technology for Life and Plant-Microbe Interactions groups, with close interaction with bioinformatics, microbial ecology and experimental crop research at the UU and NOAH partners. You will have access to Utrecht University GPU/HPC infrastructure and large, curated microbiome and microbial genome datasets. Additionally, you will  collaborate closely  with NOAH partners at Aarhus University, Niab, INRAE and The Hyve, including experimental teams that will directly test model predictions. Your qualities We are looking for a postdoctoral researcher who enjoys developing methods for complex biological data and working closely with experimental scientists. You meet  the following criteria: • a PhD, or a PhD close to completion, in machine learning, artificial intelligence, computational biology, bioinformatics, computer science or a closely related field; • strong hands-on experience with deep learning and modern representation learning, preferably including transformers, self-supervised learning, foundation models, autoencoders or related architectures; • strong programming skills in Python and experience with a deep-learning framework such as PyTorch, including training and evaluating models on GPU/HPC infrastructure; • experience working with high-dimensional biological, omics, ecological or similarly sparse and heterogeneous data, or a clear motivation to develop this expertise; • an interest in interpretable AI, rigorous benchmarking and reproducible research, together with the ability to collaborate across AI, bioinformatics, microbiology and crop science. Experience with microbiome data, microbial genomics, metagenomics or multimodal biological data is an advantage, but is not required if you bring strong machine-learning expertise and are motivated to learn the biology. Our offer a central role in developing ARCA, the core AI technology of the five-year EIC Pathfinder project NOAH; a position available from January 2027, initially for 1 year, extended with 3 years after a positive evaluation; a gross monthly salary, depending on qualifications and expereince, between €3.706 and €5.760 (salary scale 10 under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)). The salary is based on a 38-hour working week; 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment set out in our collective labour agreement, we offer attractive additional benefits, including opportunities for personal and professional growth , flexible leave arrangements, and extra vacation days. Through the UU Terms of Employment Options Model, you can tailor your employment package to your needs. In this way, we encourage you to grow in what you do, both in your work and in your development. Read more about our terms of employment . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . Working at the Faculty of Science means bringing together inspiring people across disciplines and with a variety of perspectives and backgrounds. The Faculty has six departments: Biology, Pharmaceutical Sciences, Information & Computing Sciences, Physics, Chemistry and Mathematics. Together, we work on excellent research and inspiring education. We do so, driven by curiosity and supported by outstanding infrastructure. Visit us on LinkedIn and discover how you can become part of our community. This position is embedded jointly in the AI Technology for Life group in the Department of Information and Computing Sciences, and the Plant-Microbe Interactions group in the Department of Biology. The research combines machine and deep learning (AI), microbiome biology, microbial genomics, and bioinformatics. You will work closely with Dr Ronnie de Jonge , Prof. Sanne Abeln and researchers across both groups. You will be part of NOAH, a European EIC Pathfinder consortium coordinated by Utrecht University. NOAH brings together Utrecht University, Aarhus University, Niab, INRAE and The Hyve to develop predictive and generative AI for crop microbiome engineering. The consortium connects AI model development directly to large-scale microbiome data generation, microbial culture collections, greenhouse experiments and field validation. More information For more information, please contact Dr Ronnie de Jonge at r.dejonge@uu.nl . Do you have a question about the application procedure? Please send an email to science.recruitment@uu.nl . Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Hugo R. Kruytgebouw , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page . Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. If you are enthusiastic about this position, apply via the “Apply now” button. Please enclose: your curriculum vitae, clearly demonstrating your relevant experience for this position (based on the criteria under “ Your qualities”; your letter of motivation, specifically explaining: (1) how your relevant, demonstrable expertise aligns with the project’s goals; (2) how you would approach the described task(s); the names and contact details of at least two references; a copy of your PhD certificate or a letter stating when your defence will take place. If this specific opportunity is not for you, but you know someone else who may be interested, please forward this vacancy to them. Some connections are fundamental – Be one of them #FundamentalConnection The application deadline is 17 October 2026. Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/phd-in-the-history-of-democracy-in-the-1970s": {
      "title": "PhD in the History of Democracy in the 1970s",
      "description": "PhD in the History of Democracy in the 1970s - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs PhD in the History of Democracy in the 1970s Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Open submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Open submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests PhD in the History of Democracy in the 1970s Faculty: Faculty of Humanities Department: Department of History and Art History Hours per week: 36 to 40 Application deadline: 22 October 2026 Apply now We offer a four-year, full-time PhD position as part of an NWO-funded VIDI project investigating the transformation of democracy in the final quarter of the twentieth century through the lens of media governance. We are looking for a curious, creative, and collaborative researcher with strong training in historical research methods to investigate the contestation of the public media model from below in the long 1970s. Your job The Project Across Western Europe, democracy underwent profound changes during the final decades of the twentieth century. The postwar model of democracy, characterized by institutional trust, social cohesion, and political consensus, came under pressure, giving way to a more fragmented, polarized and contested democratic order. The postwar democratic order was closely linked to a particular model of media governance, with the public service broadcasting monopoly at its core. But in subsequent decades, debates about the role, organization, and regulation of media became a key arena in which democratic ideals and institutions were contested and (re-)formed. The NWO-funded VIDI project Democracy and the (de-)regulation of the media examines democracy’s contestation and transformation in the final quarter of the twentieth century through the lens of media governance. Through a comparative Western European perspective, the project investigates how social movements, interest organisations, regulators and policy makers and their conceptions and practices of democracy shaped media governance reform from the 1970s to the 2000s. This enables us to understand the foundations of the liberalized and pluralized media model of the final quarter of the twentieth century and of the regulation of digital media which stand at the heart of concerns about democracy’s crisis today. Your Role As a PhD-researcher, you will study the first and crucial phase of the challenge to the postwar model of democracy. You will investigate how social movements, alternative media initiatives, intellectuals and cultural pioneers challenged the institutional and normative foundations of public service broadcasting. These actors offered a stark critique of government control over media based on cultural cohesion, sociopolitical consensus and popular education; and articulated new democratic ideals centring on participation, pluralism, autonomy, and freedom of expression. This contributed to broader transformations in democratic culture and governance – and to subsequent debates on media reform in political and policy circles. Focusing on the long 1970s, a period marked by contestation of established democratic norms and hierarchies, your research project will analyse how alternative visions of the relationship between media and democracy emerged from below and challenged the public media monopoly. You will trace how and why dominant conceptions and practices of media regulation came under pressure and assess how ideas and practices from below reshaped and influenced public and political debates on media policy. Through a comparative study of Western Europe, you will investigate interactions between social movements, ideas, and political change from a historical perspective. Your research will be based on extensive archival research and the critical analysis and contextualisation of primary sources from social movements, alternative media organisations, intellectuals and other relevant actors. You will examine how democratic concepts and forms of media governance evolved over time, paying particular attention to the circulation of ideas, actors and practices across national borders. As a PhD-researcher, you will be part of the project team with a Postdoc and the Project Leader. The daily supervisor of the PhD-project is Pepijn Corduwener, the Project Leader; co-promotors are Professor Liesbeth van de Grift (International History) and Professor José van Dijck (Media Studies). You will be a member of the section of Political History, at the Department of History and Art History at Utrecht University. Your qualities We are looking for a collaborative and curious researcher who wants to play an active role in our research community and who meets the following requirements: an (R)MA degree in History, or in a related discipline combined with demonstrable advanced training and experience in historical research; demonstrable experience with historical research methods, including archival research and the critical analysis and contextualisation of primary sources; a strong interest in the modern and contemporary history of democracy in Europe; professional competence in English and at least one other European language; an interest in working with diverse academic and non-academic stakeholders in a collaborative research environment. We particularly welcome candidates who have: experience with comparative or transnational historical research; worked on topics related to the history of democracy, social movements, media history and/or media governance; additional European language skills. Our offer a fulltime temporary position for 18 months, with an extension to a total of four years upon a successful assessment in the first year, and with the specific intent that it results in a doctorate within this period; a working week of 36 - 40 hours; a gross monthly salary of € 3.204 and €4.051 in the case of full-time employment (salary scale P, job profile PhD candidate (promovendus in Dutch) of the University Job Classification (UFO), under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)); 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment laid down in the CAO NU, Utrecht University also offers a range of its own schemes for employees. This includes arrangements for professional development , various types of leave, and options for sports and cultural activities . You can also tailor your employment conditions through our Terms of Employment Options Model. In this way, we encourage you to keep investing in your personal and professional development. For more information, please visit Working at Utrecht University . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . The Faculty of Humanities has around 7,000 students and 1,100 staff members. It comprises four knowledge domains: Philosophy and Religious Studies, History and Art History, Media and Culture Studies, and Languages, Literature and Communication. With its research and education in these fields, the Faculty aims to contribute to a better understanding of the Netherlands and Europe in a rapidly changing social and cultural context. The enthusiastic and committed colleagues and the excellent amenities in the historical city centre of Utrecht, where the faculty is located , contribute to an inspiring working environment. The faculty strives for diversity among its employees and students and is committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment for everyone, as can be read in Utrecht University's Equality, Diversity and Inclusion policy. The core values of the faculty for Humanities are professionalism, transparency, responsibility, connectedness and safety. These values apply to everyone and we uphold these values in our work and in our communication with each other and with others. More information For more information about this position, please contact the project-leader of the VIDI-project, dr. Pepijn Corduwener at p.corduwener@uu.nl Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. external link If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Drift 6 external link , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page external link . Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. To apply, please send your curriculum vitae, including a letter of motivation, via the ‘apply now’ button. your curriculum vitae; a research proposal of max. two pages that serves as a starting point for the interview in which you indicate relevant potential questions, case studies and source material based on the project description above; a writing sample in the form of either your MA-thesis or an MA-research paper that demonstrate your experience with primary sources research; the names and contact details of two references; a motivation letter of max 1 page. The first round will take place online on 2 November and the second round will take place in person in Utrecht on 9 November. The application deadline is 22 October 2026. Apply now Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/postdoc-co-creating-shared-narratives-for-sustainable-sediment-management": {
      "title": "Postdoc: Co-creating Shared Narratives for Sustainable Sediment Management",
      "description": "Postdoc: Co-creating Shared Narratives for Sustainable Sediment Management - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs Postdoc: Co-creating Shared Narratives for Sustainable Sediment Management Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Close submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Close submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests Postdoc: Co-creating Shared Narratives for Sustainable Sediment Management Faculty: Faculty of Geosciences Department: Department of Physical Geography Hours per week: 32 to 40 Application deadline: 25 October 2026 The Wadden Sea and the Ems-Dollard and Western Scheldt estuaries have nationally important navigation channels and globally important ecosystems on tidal flats and coastal wetlands, that also protect the coast against storms. However, dredging costs are escalating, biodiversity is declining, and the sea level is rising. These three threats are magnified by a surplus or deficit of sand and mud, which requires sediment management. The knowledge and tools needed for this pose profound scientific challenges. Your job The aim of the WadSED project is to develop the urgently needed system understanding, data analysis tools, and forecasting models for science and expert users, and co-create common ground among stakeholders for future-proof coastal management. One the project outcomes is to explore and co-develop narratives and conceptual models that show development of the basins and estuaries under SLR and human interference with specific attention for the sediment budget and habitat development. As a postdoctoral researcher you will organize and facilitate the participatory process to co-develop these shared narratives and conceptual models for sustainable sediment management. This will be done for two case studies, namely Western Scheldt and Wadden Sea. To facilitate knowledge exchange and the learning process, interactive workshops will be organized on location (Western Scheldt and Wadden Sea) to enhance science-society interactions. Responsibilities The primary responsibilities of the postdoctoral researcher are to engage with the project team members and the participating experts and stakeholders for both case studies to coordinate, facilitate and monitor the co-creation process to develop shared narratives and conceptual model for sustainable sediment management. This will be achieved through: Designing and organizing a deliberative and participatory approach together with the WadSED team members (specifically UU and HVHL). Facilitating and monitoring the co-creation process with experts and stakeholders in Western Scheldt and Wadden Sea. Applying a research for policy cycle strategy to co-create new conceptual models with academic scholars, experts, stakeholders and policy makers. Facilitating knowledge exchange between researchers, experts, stakeholders and policymakers through stakeholder workshops. Disseminating best practices via communication strategies that target experts, policy makers, and the public. The selected candidate is expected to contribute to the above-mentioned tasks in collaboration with the rest of the WadSED team members. Your qualities We are searching for a candidate with the following qualifications: Fluency in Dutch and English; A PhD degree in social and environmental policy sciences or related disciplines; Expertise and experience working in facilitating deliberative and participatory processes in social- ecological systems; Affinity with the water management sector; Demonstrable skills in qualitative research and scientific writing; Strong communication skills and preferably expertise with learning design (for example visual media production project); Knowledge of mediated participation in a context of natural resource management transitions is an advantage; Cross-cultural sensitivity and a team player attitude, willing to be part of a large national; A driving license might come in handy. Our offer We offer: a job (0.8 - 1.0 FTE) for 24 months; a working week of 32 - 40 hours and a gross monthly salary between €3.706 and €5.760 (salary scale 10 under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)). The salary is based on a 38-hour working week; 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment set out in our collective labour agreement, we offer attractive additional benefits, including opportunities for personal and professional growth , flexible leave arrangements, and extra vacation days. Through the UU Terms of Employment Options Model, you can tailor your employment package to your needs. In this way, we encourage you to grow in what you do, both in your work and in your development. Read more about our terms of employment . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . Utrecht University’s Faculty of Geosciences studies the Earth: from the Earth’s core to its surface, including man’s spatial and material utilisation of the Earth – always with a focus on sustainability and innovation. With 3,400 students (BSc and MSc) and 720 staff, the faculty is a strong and challenging organisation. The Faculty of Geosciences is organised in four Departments: Earth Sciences, Human Geography & Spatial Planning, Physical Geography, and Sustainable Development. More information For more information, please contact Dr Jan Fliervoet via jan.fliervoet@hvhl.nl Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Vening Meineszgebouw A , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page . To apply, please send your curriculum vitae, including a letter of motivation, via the ‘apply now’ button. The application deadline is 25 October 2026. Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/junior-research-assistant-in-research-and-reproducibility": {
      "title": "Junior Research Assistant in Research and Reproducibility",
      "description": "Junior Research Assistant in Research and Reproducibility - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs Junior Research Assistant in Research and Reproducibility Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Close submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Close submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests Junior Research Assistant in Research and Reproducibility Faculty: Faculty of Social and Behavioural Sciences Department: Social Sciences Hours per week: 20 to 24 Application deadline: 4 October 2026 Do you combine strong R skills with the ability to bring structure to complex collaborative research? The Psychology of Political Behavior Studies (PPBS) is looking for a senior research assistant to independently operate our reproducible data and reporting pipeline, maintain our website and shared research infrastructure, and turn meetings and decisions into reliable follow-through. You will work within an international political psychology programme under the direction of Dr Flavio Azevedo and Jasmin Haggemann. Your job The Psychology of Political Behavior Studies (PPBS; ppbs.science ) is a longitudinal research programme examining political attitudes, identities, behaviour, and psychological constructs across multiple survey waves, samples, panel providers, and countries. PPBS maintains linked codebooks, data-processing workflows, reproducible reports, a public website, and shared research and project-management infrastructure. As Senior Research Assistant in Research Operations and Reproducibility, you will ensure that this infrastructure is organised, documented, reproducible, and moving forward. You will work under the direction of Dr Flavio Azevedo and Jasmin Haggemann. You will independently own the technical operation of the pipeline, while scientific, measurement, ethical, and strategic decisions remain with the supervisors and relevant PPBS researchers. Your core tasks include: running, maintaining, debugging, documenting, and incrementally improving PPBS workflows in R and R Markdown or Quarto; managing the progression from raw survey exports to cleaned, labelled, processed, validated, and versioned analysis-ready datasets without compromising source-data integrity; maintaining reproducible construct reports with descriptive statistics, scale reliability, tables, figures, and transparent analytical documentation; implementing and checking variable recoding, value labels, missing-data rules, reverse scoring, derived variables, composite scores, and cross-wave harmonisation; generating and maintaining empirical codebooks, data dictionaries, metadata outputs, construct specifications, and validation checks; maintaining the PPBS website repository and publication workflow in GitHub, including routine content and report updates and diagnosis of build or deployment problems; organising research files and documentation across Dropbox, Google Drive, and GitHub, with clear provenance, naming, versioning, access, and release status; preparing meeting agendas and minutes, recording decisions and dependencies, maintaining action trackers, coordinating hand-offs, and following up with contributors; and identifying operational and reproducibility risks early, proposing proportionate solutions, and escalating decisions that require scientific or managerial authority. Because the appointment is short and part-time, the immediate priority is reliable pipeline and report ownership, followed by operational coordination and routine website maintenance. Major software redevelopment or independent statistical research is outside the role unless separately agreed and resourced. Your qualities We believe in a balance between individual and collective achievements. That is why we assess both individual and team performance. We encourage staff members to share scientific knowledge, document their work transparently, and contribute to a constructive and inclusive working environment. We are looking for a systematic and dependable colleague who can work independently on technical operations while collaborating closely on scientific decisions. You meet the following requirements: you have completed a Master’s degree in social science, data science, research methods, statistics, or a related field, or demonstrate an equivalent level of professional expertise; you have at least three years of relevant experience after your Master’s degree, or equivalent professional experience, in research data, reproducible reporting, research software, or closely related work; you can independently understand, run, debug, and improve a substantial inherited workflow in R; you have strong working knowledge of R Markdown and/or Quarto, tidy data workflows, data validation, and automated report generation;you have experience cleaning, recoding, labelling, documenting, and quality-checking complex survey or social-science data; you are competent with Git and GitHub, including branches, commits, pull requests, conflict resolution, issues, and collaborative review; you can understand and maintain a research website built from a version-controlled repository; you have strong project-management habits, including agenda preparation, minute-taking, task tracking, prioritisation, and dependable follow-through; your written and spoken English enables you to turn complex or ambiguous discussions into precise records, specifications, and implementation steps; and you exercise sound judgement about when to solve an operational problem independently and when to escalate a scientific, measurement, ethical, or strategic decision. The following experience is an advantage: political psychology, psychometrics, scale construction, reliability analysis, survey methodology, or longitudinal data harmonisation; reproducible environment management, automated testing, continuous integration, dependency management, or workflow tools such as targets; metadata standards, FAIR principles, data repositories, licensing, disclosure control, or research-data governance; HTML/CSS, static-site systems, accessible web content, or web deployment workflows; responsible use of AI-assisted coding tools such as Codex or Claude, including verification of generated code and appropriate handling of sensitive information; and coordination of distributed, international, interdisciplinary, or volunteer-based research collaborations. Our offer a job for for approximately 2.5 months; the exact duration will be determined after the appropriate salary classification; a working week of 20 - 24 hours and a gross monthly salary between €3.202 and €4.159 (salary scale 7 under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU). The salary is based on a 38-hour working week; 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment set out in our collective labour agreement, we offer attractive additional benefits, including opportunities for personal and professional growth , flexible leave arrangements, and extra vacation days. Through the UU Terms of Employment Options Model, you can tailor your employment package to your needs. In this way, we encourage you to grow in what you do, both in your work and in your development. Read more about our terms of employment . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . The Faculty of Social and Behavioural Sciences is one of the leading faculties in Europe providing research and academic teaching in cultural anthropology, educational sciences, interdisciplinary social science, pedagogical sciences, psychology, and sociology. Almost 7,000 students are enrolled in a broad range of undergraduate and graduate programmes. The Faculty of Social and Behavioural Sciences has some 1,100 faculty and staff members, all providing their individual contribution to the training and education of young talent and to the research into and finding solutions for scientific and societal issues. The faculty is located at Utrecht Science Park near the historical city centre of Utrecht. More information For more information, please contact Dr Flavio Azevedo at f.a.azevedo@uu.nl Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Sjoerd Groenmangebouw , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page . Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. To apply, please send your curriculum vitae, including a letter of motivation, via the ‘apply now’ button. The selection process will include a short practical exercise using a small, non-sensitive PPBS-style dataset and repository. The exercise will assess your ability to understand inherited R code, identify reproducibility risks, implement and validate a limited change, render a report, use Git appropriately, and explain your decisions clearly. The application deadline is 5 October 2026. Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/postdoc-machine-learning-for-wind-flow-prediction-in-coastal-dunes": {
      "title": "Postdoc: Machine learning for wind flow prediction in coastal dunes",
      "description": "Postdoc: Machine learning for wind flow prediction in coastal dunes - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs Postdoc: Machine learning for wind flow prediction in coastal dunes Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Close submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Close submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests Postdoc: Machine learning for wind flow prediction in coastal dunes Faculty: Faculty of Geosciences Department: Department of Physical Geography Hours per week: 36 to 40 Application deadline: 6 November 2026 Coastal dunes are dynamic landforms that provide flood protection and crucial habitat in the Netherlands and across the world. Coastal dunes are formed by sand transport, which strongly relies on the interaction between wind and dune shape. Recent improvements in airflow modelling and coastal dune modelling are promising. However, getting accurate wind field predictions remains challenging, especially in dunes with complex topography. Your job In this 2-year postdoc position, you will use numerical modelling and machine learning techniques to increase the accuracy of coastal dune models. As a result, your project will inform and enhance decision-making in coastal dune management. The ultimate goal of this project is to build a surrogate wind field model that can feed accurate wind field predictions into numerical coastal dune models. You will start by evaluating current wind field predictions from the coastal dune model AeoLiS by comparing model output with existing field measurements and/or Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) simulations. Specifically, you will assess where simplified assumptions in the model fall short. You will then train a machine learning model (such as PySR or neural networks) on CFD data to develop a fast, data-driven wind field predictor. You will combine this surrogate model with AeoLiS and evaluate the accuracy of the new model setup by applying it to existing case studies. Where needed, you will contribute to field data collection to support model validation. You will share your results in stakeholder meetings, scientific conferences, and academic journals. Your qualities You have a strong background in data science and experience applying machine learning and other AI techniques. You are interested in applying these techniques to physical systems, in this case, to coastal dunes, wind flow dynamics and wind-driven sand transport. You have an open, collaborative and curious attitude. You enjoy exploring new approaches and you combine this with the pragmatism that is needed to move a project forward. By the time the position starts, you have obtained a PhD degree in Data Sciences, Computer Sciences, Physics, Earth Sciences, Civil Engineering, or a related field. You have a strong background in numerical modelling and programming (preferably Python). You will collaborate with colleagues who are AeoLiS and CFD specialists, so affinity with these techniques is a plus but not required. Affinity with the collection and analysis of (aeolian) field measurements is a plus. You communicate clearly and have a strong command of the English language. Our offer We offer: a position (1.0 FTE) for 24 months. a working week of 36 - 40 hours and a gross monthly salary between €3.706 and €5.760 (salary scale 10 under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)). The salary is based on a 38-hour working week; 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment set out in our collective labour agreement, we offer attractive additional benefits, including opportunities for personal and professional growth , flexible leave arrangements, and extra vacation days. Through the UU Terms of Employment Options Model, you can tailor your employment package to your needs. In this way, we encourage you to grow in what you do, both in your work and in your development. Read more about our terms of employment . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . Utrecht University’s Faculty of Geosciences studies the Earth: from the Earth’s core to its surface, including man’s spatial and material utilisation of the Earth – always with a focus on sustainability and innovation. With 3,400 students (BSc and MSc) and 720 staff, the faculty is a strong and challenging organisation. The Faculty of Geosciences is organised in four Departments: Earth Sciences, Human Geography & Spatial Planning, Physical Geography, and Sustainable Development. More information For more information about this position, please contact Dr Christa van IJzendoorn at c.o.vanijzendoorn@uu.nl . Please do not send your application to this email address but use the ‘Apply Now’ button. Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Vening Meineszgebouw A , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page . Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. To apply, please send the following documents via the 'apply now' button: Your curriculum vitae (max 2 pages), including relevant research experience, publications and work carried out during your PhD and/or previous postdoctoral positions. Your motivation letter (max 1 page), in which you show your enthusiasm for the topic of this position, describe why this position fits your background and interests, and explain how your expertise would contribute to the project. Please also explain why you are interested in working at Utrecht University and within the Department of Physical Geography. The names and contact details of at least two references The first interview is online via MS Teams in the week of 23 November. If applicable, the second interview is in the week of 30 November. If you have an international (non-Dutch) Master’s diploma, you will be requested to provide your Bachelor’s and Master’s diplomas as well as the corresponding grade lists (in English) if you are selected for this position. The preferred start date is 1 January 2027, but this is flexible. Regardless of the start date, the contract needs to end at or before 31 December 2028. This means that if the contract starts later than 1 January 2027, it will be shorter than 2 years; if it starts earlier, it may be longer than 2 years (depending on available funding). Note that international candidates that need a visa/work permit for the Netherlands require at least four months processing time after selection and acceptance. This will be arranged with help of the International Service Desk (ISD) of our university. Finding appropriate housing in or near Utrecht is your own responsibility, but the ISD may be able to advise you therewith. Unfortunately, we must warn that it is a tight market at the moment. In case of general questions about working and living in The Netherlands, please consult the Dutch Mobility Portal . The application deadline is 7 November 2026. Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/postdoc-ocean-acidification-the-marine-phosphorus-cycle": {
      "title": "Postdoc: Ocean Acidification & the Marine Phosphorus Cycle",
      "description": "Postdoc: Ocean Acidification & the Marine Phosphorus Cycle - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs Postdoc: Ocean Acidification & the Marine Phosphorus Cycle Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Close submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Close submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests Postdoc: Ocean Acidification & the Marine Phosphorus Cycle Faculty: Faculty of Geosciences Department: Department of Earth Sciences Hours per week: 36 to 40 Application deadline: 31 October 2026 Microscopic calcite (CaCO3) shells of plankton sink through ocean waters to the seafloor by the trillions each year, taking along essential nutrients such as phosphorus (P). Ocean acidification is likely to alter global CaCO3 fluxes, with so far unknown consequences for P cycling, ocean fertility and CO2 uptake. Our lack of knowledge regarding these feedbacks limits our insight in ocean functioning under environmental change, both in the (ancient) past and the human-impacted future. Your job The NWO-funded PHOSFLUX project aims to shed light on these unknowns, by quantifying the role of CaCO3 fluxes and acidification in global marine P cycling through a combination of field work in the North Atlantic Ocean, laboratory experiments and numerical modelling. A scientific cruise with our research vessel Pelagia will allow us to investigate CaCO3 – P interactions in the field. Through controlled lab experiments, we will dive deeper into the underlying chemical and mineralogical processes, to eventually capture them in physical-chemical models that predict the impact of acidification on marine P cycling. The PHOSFLUX project is a collaboration between the NIOZ (dr. Peter Kraal) and Utrecht University (UU; prof. dr. Mariëtte Wolthers), combining unique sea-going and on-shore experimentation with advanced analytical tools to understand mineral-water interactions from microscopic to ocean-basin scale. Besides PI’s Kraal and Wolthers, the PHOSFLUX team consists of a NIOZ-based PhD student performing experiments and analyses, and a UU-based postdoctoral researcher who focuses on modelling the CaCO3 – P interactions. As postdoctoral researcher within PHOSFLUX, your role is to model P dynamics in sinking and deposited marine snow in relation to changing environmental conditions in the water column. To achieve this, you will create a reactive-transport model for P dynamics that will include processes such as CaCO3 dissolution, organic matter decomposition and adsorption/incorporation of PO4 into solid phases of marine snow. You will test the performance of your model by simulating experimental and field data in collaboration with the PHOSFLUX PhD student. Your qualities We are looking for a highly motivated postdoctoral candidate with a background in biogeochemical modelling. You must have successfully completed a PhD programme in Earth Sciences, Environmental Sciences, Biology or a related discipline by the time the position starts. Preferably, you also have: Experience and affinity with biogeochemistry and marine research; Experience in numerical modelling of mineral-water interactions and/or organic matter degradation processes; Experience or strong interest in synchrotron-based analysis techniques You approach scientific problems with determination and are eager to develop multidisciplinary skills. You must be willing to travel abroad to join the PHOSFLUX team during synchrotron beamtimes and to present your research to an international research community. As this project requires close collaboration within the research team, candidates are expected to be excellent team players. You also have excellent written and spoken English skills and good general communicational skills. We highly encourage applicants from diverse backgrounds to join us. Our offer We offer: a position (1.0 FTE) for 36 months. a working week of 36 - 40 hours and a gross monthly salary between €3.706 and €5.760 (salary scale 10 under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)). The salary is based on a 38-hour working week; 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment set out in our collective labour agreement, we offer attractive additional benefits, including opportunities for personal and professional growth , flexible leave arrangements, and extra vacation days. Through the UU Terms of Employment Options Model, you can tailor your employment package to your needs. In this way, we encourage you to grow in what you do, both in your work and in your development. Read more about our terms of employment . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . Utrecht University’s Faculty of Geosciences studies the Earth: from the Earth’s core to its surface, including man’s spatial and material utilisation of the Earth – always with a focus on sustainability and innovation. With 3,400 students (BSc and MSc) and 720 staff, the faculty is a strong and challenging organisation. The Faculty of Geosciences is organised in four Departments: Earth Sciences, Human Geography & Spatial Planning, Physical Geography, and Sustainable Development. The Department of Earth Sciences conducts teaching and research across the full range of the solid Earth and environmental Earth sciences, with activities in almost all areas of geology, geochemistry, geophysics, biogeology and hydrogeology. The department hosts a highly international tenured staff of over 50 scientists and more than 110 PhD students and postdoctoral researchers. We house or have access to a wide variety of world-class laboratories, among which are UU’s Electron Microscopy Centre , the Geolab , and the Earth Simulation Lab . We also have excellent High- Performance Computing facilities. About Utrecht The department and its facilities are located at Utrecht Science Park. Utrecht is the fourth largest city in the Netherlands with a population of nearly 360,000 and forms a hub in the middle of the country. Its historical city centre and its modern central station can easily be reached from the Science Park by public transport or by a 15-minute bicycle ride. Utrecht boasts beautiful canals with extraordinary wharf cellars housing cafés and terraces by the water, as well as a broad variety of shops and boutiques. More information For informal information (not for application) about this position, please contact Prof. Dr Mariëtte Wolthers at m.wolthers@uu.nl Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Vening Meineszgebouw A , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page . Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. To apply, please send your curriculum vitae, a letter of motivation, and the (e-mail) addresses of at least two referees via the 'apply now' button. E-mail applications cannot be accepted. We aim for a quick selection procedure after the application deadline. The position can start as soon as possible and ultimately by 1 January 2027. Note that international candidates that need a visa/work permit for the Netherlands require at least four months processing time after selection and acceptance. This will be arranged with help of the International Service Desk (ISD) of our university. Finding appropriate housing in or near Utrecht is your own responsibility, but the ISD may be able to advise you therewith. In case of general questions about working and living in The Netherlands, please consult the Dutch Mobility Portal . The application deadline is 1 November 2026. Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/assistant-professor-in-applied-mathematics": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Applied Mathematics",
      "description": "Assistant Professor in Applied Mathematics - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs Assistant Professor in Applied Mathematics Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Close submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Close submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests Assistant Professor in Applied Mathematics Faculty: Faculty of Science Department: Department of Mathematics Hours per week: 36 to 40 Application deadline: 31 October 2026 Join the Mathematical Institute of Utrecht University! As Assistant Professor in Applied Mathematics. you’ll develop your research programme, inspire students and advance education. Collaborating closely with colleagues and students, you’ll apply mathematics to tackle societal challenges and bring new data-driven modelling, experiment, and computer-aided mathematics into education. We welcome applicants from all areas of applied mathematics. Your job Working in Applied Mathematics within the Mathematical Institute means contributing to research that connects mathematics with the wider sciences, from life sciences and medical imaging to climate and ocean dynamics, and to teaching in which students meet current research first-hand. As a team, we aim to develop models and methods that are mathematically sound, computationally powerful and useful to other disciplines, and to prepare students to use new tools with confidence and care. Our societal impact is reflected in close collaborations with partners such as UMC Utrecht, RIVM and CWI (see below for more), and in work on pressing problems such as the spread of diseases and changing climate. We are looking for an assistant professor (UD) in applied mathematics who can strengthen our Mathematical modelling research group, both substantively and strategically. We are open to candidates with an interdisciplinary research focus in mathematical modelling with a strong orientation toward real-world applications, as well as to candidates who can contribute to developing a sustainable vision on the role and impact of AI within mathematical research. As an assistant professor in applied mathematics, you will balance research and teaching (approximately 50% each) in a role that allows you to grow as a leader while making a real impact on society. In your research, you will develop your own line, driven by real-world problems. You’ll collaborate with scientists across disciplines, securing funding and building partnerships to amplify your impact. And as a mentor and supervisor, you’ll guide the next generation of mathematicians, including PhD candidates and postdocs. In teaching, you’ll have the opportunity to teach and develop courses at every level, from core first-year Bachelor’s courses to advanced Master’s courses. Master’s courses can be taught either at the local level or as part of the national master programme, Mastermath. You’ll also supervise theses and smaller-scale projects. With university funding available for innovation, you’ll play a key role in defining the future of our curriculum, ensuring it remains resilient and adaptive in the face of fast-changing research methods. By getting involved in the daily life of the Mathematical Institute , you’ll play an active and committed role in the academic life of our department and faculty. In addition, you help shape the future of the department, bringing fresh ideas and energy to our community. Your qualities We are looking for a mathematician who brings a combination of the following qualifications, experience and potential: a PhD in mathematics or closely related field; a solid research foundation for your career stage, with peer-reviewed publications and other meaningful research contributions, paired with the drive to lead independent research; capacity to develop competitive funding proposals and foster research collaborations; experience or demonstrable affinity with modelling, analysis and/or computational experimentation applied to real-world problems; proven ability or potential to supervise and mentor postdocs, PhD candidates, Bachelor's and Master's students with enthusiasm and dedication; a commitment to inspiring and engaging teaching and the ability to teach courses across the breadth of the core Bachelor programme up to specialist Master's courses in Mathematics and related disciplines, based on an in-depth understanding of current developments in the field; engagement with international and national scientific and societal communities; the willingness to obtaining the University Teaching Qualification (UTQ) if you do not yet have it (support provided). proficiency in English (C1 level). Dutch is not required at the start, but we do ask you to learn the language within 5 years (support provided). We recognise that academics follow diverse career paths and that no list of qualifications can capture every strength. We are committed to improving gender balance and fostering an inclusive academic community in which colleagues can develop and thrive. If this role aligns with your experience and aspirations, we encourage you to apply—even if you do not meet every qualification. Applications from groups currently underrepresented in mathematics are particularly welcome. Our offer a position for one year, with the prospect of permanent employment following a positive evaluation; a gross monthly salary, depending on qualifications and experience, between €4.925 and €6.682 (salary scale 11 under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)). The salary is based on a 38-hour working week; 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment set out in our collective labour agreement, we offer attractive additional benefits, including opportunities for personal and professional growth , flexible leave arrangements, and extra vacation days. Through the UU Terms of Employment Options Model, you can tailor your employment package to your needs. In this way, we encourage you to grow in what you do, both in your work and in your development. Read more about our terms of employment . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . Working at the Faculty of Science means bringing together inspiring people across disciplines and with a variety of perspectives and backgrounds. The Faculty has six departments: Biology, Pharmaceutical Sciences, Information & Computing Sciences, Physics, Chemistry and Mathematics. Together, we work on excellent research and inspiring education. We do so, driven by curiosity and supported by outstanding infrastructure. Visit us on LinkedIn and discover how you can become part of our community. The Mathematical Institute of the Department of Mathematics organises and teaches the Bachelor's curricula in Mathematics as well as the (English-taught) Master's programme in Mathematics and some service teaching in mathematics. The Institute is internationally recognised for its research in both pure and applied mathematics. It houses the Utrecht Geometry Centre and Mathematical modelling group and maintains a long-standing tradition of transdisciplinary collaboration with other scientific fields including, among others, atmosphere/ocean sciences, computational life sciences, mathematical biology, mathematical economics, medical and seismic imaging, theoretical computer science, and theoretical physics. We participate in university focus areas in Complex Systems and Applied Data Science. Funds are available for accessing national supercomputer centre SURF. You will be part of the Mathematical modelling group , where collaboration across disciplines and sectors is an important part of our research environment. As a part of the Mathematical Institute, the group has close ties with the Departments of Biology, Physics, Information and Computer Sciences, the Faculty of Geosciences, UMCU (Utrecht University Medical Center). And also with companies such as Rabobank, European Investment Bank (EIB), ASML, TNO and Canon, and institutes such as KNMI (Royal Netherlands Meteorological Institute), RIVM (The Dutch National Institute for Public Health and the Environment) and CWI (the National Research Institute for Mathematics and Computer Science). These connections create opportunities to develop interdisciplinary research and engage with academic, public-sector and industry partners. More information For more information, please contact Prof. Jason Frank (chair of the selection committee) at j.e.frank@uu.nl . Do you have a question about the application procedure? Please send an email to science.recruitment@uu.nl . Timeline: The in-person interviews are scheduled for the week of January 11, 2027. In case of a large number of applications, a first round of online interviews will be scheduled on November 23, 2026. The preferred starting date is September 1, 2027 or earlier. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. If you find this position attractive, apply via the \"Apply now\" button! Please enclose: your letter of motivation; your curriculum vitae, including a publication list; a research statement (at most three pages (A4), including background, best accomplishments, and future plans, as well as ideas you may have on the use of AI-based methods in research); a teaching statement (at most two pages (A4), including teaching vision, best accomplishments, and future plans, as well as ideas you may have on introducing AI-based methods into the curriculum); the names and email addresses of three contacts whom we can ask for a reference letter, at least one of whom can address teaching abilities. Utrecht University endorses the principles of Recognition and Rewards . The Utrecht TRIPLE model is a tool for applying the basic principles of Recognition and Rewards in practice. This model recognises six dimensions of academic performance: Team spirit, Research, Impact, Professional performance, Leadership and Education. It helps to consider the various aspects of the academic work from a broad perspective and relate them to each other. If this specific opportunity isn’t for you, but you know someone else who may be interested, please forward this vacancy to them. Some connections are fundamental – Be one of them #FundamentalConnection The application deadline is 31 October 2026. Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/phd-position-on-biophysical-origins-of-rna-toxicity-in-huntingtons-disease": {
      "title": "PhD Position on Biophysical Origins of RNA Toxicity in Huntington’s Disease",
      "description": "PhD Position on Biophysical Origins of RNA Toxicity in Huntington’s Disease - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs PhD Position on Biophysical Origins of RNA Toxicity in Huntington’s Disease Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Close submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Close submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests PhD Position on Biophysical Origins of RNA Toxicity in Huntington’s Disease Faculty: Faculty of Science Department: Department of Chemistry Hours per week: 36 to 40 Application deadline: 31 October 2026 In Huntington’s Disease (HD), expansion of CAG repeats in the huntingtin (HTT) gene leads to a toxic gain-of-function that ultimately results in fatal neurodegeneration. HD research has long focused on the toxic effects of the HTT-encoded polyglutamine protein that aggregates in neurons. However, recent discoveries reveal that the HTT mRNA itself forms condensates and aggregates, contributing to neuronal toxicity. In this project, your work will help define the origins of RNA toxicity in HD - a critical step toward developing therapies. Your job As a PhD candidate in this project, you will design, execute, and analyze single-molecule experiments using optical tweezers and confocal fluorescence imaging, building on previous work (O’Brien et al., Nat Comm 2024). Together with our collaborators, you will produce materials for these experiments and develop novel approaches for protein and RNA labeling. Working at the interface of biophysics and biochemistry, you will establish new paradigms that explain why some repeat RNAs are toxic while others are not. Your responsibilities include: Independently conducting experimental work and performing data analysis; Presenting your progress in regular team meetings; Publishing your findings in peer-reviewed journals; Participating in scientific conferences; Supporting teaching and mentoring undergraduate and master's students. Under the mentorship of Prof. Christian Kaiser, you will have the freedom to explore your own ideas within the project goals. You will work closely with junior investigators in the group as well as with our local and international collaborators, and engage with the broader research community at the Bijvoet Centre and the Faculty of Science. Visit the Kaiser Lab and the Bijvoet Centre websites for more information. Your qualities You are genuinely curious and passionate about science, motivated to work independently, and eager to understand biology at the molecular and biophysical level. You have strong critical thinking and problem-solving skills, and you bring: An MSc degree in (bio)physics, (bio)chemistry, or a related field; Experience with wet lab work and data analysis; Strong scientific writing and communication skills; Proficiency in written and spoken English; Experience with single-molecule approaches (not required, but a plus). Our offer a position for 18 months, with an extension to a total of four years upon successful assessment in the first 18 months; a full-time working week and a gross monthly salary between €3.204 and €4.051 (salary scale P under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)). The salary is based on a 38-hour working week; 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment set out in our collective labour agreement, we offer attractive additional benefits, including opportunities for personal and professional growth , flexible leave arrangements, and extra vacation days. Through the UU Terms of Employment Options Model, you can tailor your employment package to your needs. This way, we encourage you to grow in what you do, both in your work and your development. Read more about our terms of employment . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . Working at the Faculty of Science means bringing together inspiring people across disciplines and with a variety of perspectives and backgrounds. The Faculty has six departments: Biology, Pharmaceutical Sciences, Information & Computing Sciences, Physics, Chemistry and Mathematics. Together, we work on excellent research and inspiring education. We do so, driven by curiosity and supported by outstanding infrastructure. Visit us on LinkedIn and discover how you can become part of our community. You will work in an interdisciplinary research team led by Prof. Christian Kaiser. The lab is situated within the Molecular Biophysics and Biochemistry group, which employs diverse approaches ranging from cell biology methods to (bio)chemical and biophysical techniques, with systems ranging from self-assembled model structures to yeast and from bacterial and mammalian cells to the worm C. elegans. The group is part of the Bijvoet Centre for Biomolecular Research ( Department of Chemistry ), which offers world-class expertise and infrastructure for various life science areas, including protein biochemistry and biophysics, mass spectrometry, and structural biology. More information For more information, please contact Prof. Dr. Christian Kaiser at c.m.kaiser@uu.nl or visit the lab or group websites. Do you have a question about the application procedure? Please send an email to science.recruitment@uu.nl . Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Hugo R. Kruytgebouw , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page . If you are enthusiastic about this position, just apply via the \"Apply now\" button! Please enclose: your letter of motivation; your Curriculum vitae; the names and contact details of at least two references. A knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures for academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. To apply, please send your curriculum vitae, including a letter of motivation, via the ‘apply now’ button. The application deadline is 31 October 2026. Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/postdoc-on-geodata-science-and-geoai": {
      "title": "Postdoc on Geodata Science and GeoAI",
      "description": "Postdoc on Geodata Science and GeoAI - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs Postdoc on Geodata Science and GeoAI Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Close submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Close submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests Postdoc on Geodata Science and GeoAI Faculty: Faculty of Geosciences Department: Department of Human Geography and Spatial Planning Hours per week: 36 to 40 Application deadline: 15 November 2026 Interested in how geospatial data science and GeoAI can be applied to investigate health system resilience under the rising pressure of polycrisis? Excited about developing spatial models and GeoAI systems to create resilient health systems and reduce health disparities? Your job At Utrecht University, you will be part of the Health Geography group as a postdoctoral researcher. You will harmonize diverse spatial data and develop geospatial analytical models and GeoAI approaches, notably on research related to health system resilience. You will work closely with an interdisciplinary team to investigate how geodatabases and spatial models can be combined with other approaches such as participatory engagements to reduce health system vulnerabilities and spatial disparities in health care provision. Your main responsibilities include: integrating multi-source datasets, including GIS data, and qualitative insights; developing reproducible spatial analytical and GeoAI pipelines to investigate health system resilience and health disparities; designing scenario-based spatial models to investigate future risks and resilience of the health system and health disparities under polycrisis; evaluating and improving model performance, explainability, fairness, and robustness; collaborating with health system experts and practitioners in participatory workshops; developing an interactive, publicly accessible map-based web interface; publishing high-quality scientific articles in international journals; presenting findings at conferences and stakeholder events; contributing to supervision of MSc students. Your qualities We are looking for an enthusiastic and collaborative researcher with a strong technical background and an interest in societal impact. You have: a PhD in GIScience, geoinformatics, spatial data science, spatial analytics, or a related field; experience with diverse spatial analytical methods; strong programming skills (e.g., Python) and version control (e.g., GitHub); experience working with multi-source geospatial data (e.g., network, satellite); experience with GIS and GeoAI-related model development and evaluation; an interest in GIScience, health inequality and health system resilience; strong communication and academic writing skills; the ability to work independently as well as collaboratively in interdisciplinary teams. Experience with one or more of the following is considered an advantage: Familiarity with Dutch data portals (e.g., PDOK, CBS); WebGIS application development Conducting participatory workshops and scenario-based games (e.g., serious games). Our offer We offer: a position (1.0 FTE) for 12 months. After a positive evaluation, the position can be extended to 22 months; a working week of 36 - 40 hours and a gross monthly salary between €3.706 and €5.760 (salary scale 10 under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)). The salary is based on a 38-hour working week; 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment set out in our collective labour agreement, we offer attractive additional benefits, including opportunities for personal and professional growth , flexible leave arrangements, and extra vacation days. Through the UU Terms of Employment Options Model, you can tailor your employment package to your needs. In this way, we encourage you to grow in what you do, both in your work and in your development. Read more about our terms of employment . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . Utrecht University’s Faculty of Geosciences studies the Earth: from the Earth’s core to its surface, including man’s spatial and material utilisation of the Earth – always with a focus on sustainability and innovation. With 3,400 students (BSc and MSc) and 720 staff, the faculty is a strong and challenging organisation. The Faculty of Geosciences is organised in four Departments: Earth Sciences, Human Geography & Spatial Planning, Physical Geography, and Sustainable Development. More information For more information, please contact SM Labib at s.m.labib@uu.nl Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Vening Meineszgebouw A , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page . Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. To apply, please send,  via the ‘apply now’ button: a motivation letter (max. 1.5 pages); your curriculum vitae; contact details of two referees; one representative publication or project example. Interviews will take place late November, 2026. The start date is January 1, 2027. The application deadline is 15 November 2026. Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/postdoctoral-researcher-inclusive-governance-for-the-prince-claus-chair": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher ‘Inclusive Governance’ for the Prince Claus Chair",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Researcher ‘Inclusive Governance’ for the Prince Claus Chair - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs Postdoctoral Researcher ‘Inclusive Governance’ for the Prince Claus Chair Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Close submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Close submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests Postdoctoral Researcher ‘Inclusive Governance’ for the Prince Claus Chair Faculty: Faculty of Law, Economics and Governance Department: School of Governance Hours per week: 28 to 40 Application deadline: 1 November 2026 Institutions for Open Societies (IOS), in collaboration with Utrecht School of Governance, Faculty of Law, Economics & Governance, are honored with the appointment of Prof. Ine Apapoe (Anton de Kom University) as the Prince Claus Chair under the theme: Equity from Within: Maroon Governance and the Future of Development in Suriname. To support this appointment, we are seeking a postdoctoral researcher, based in Utrecht, for the period February 2027 until January 2029. The postdoctoral researcher is co-funded by Utrecht University Fund and IOS. Your job In this role, you will combine theoretical and methodological expertise in research with organizational and social skills to advance the goals of the Prince Claus Chair (approximately 50-50%). The postdoc position is designed to enhance high-quality academic scholarship and leverage evidence-based research for equity and development, with an emphasis on Suriname and the advancement of Inclusive Governance in Suriname. You will work closely with the Prince Claus chairholder, who will stay in Suriname and Utrecht on an alternating basis. The postdoctoral researcher will play a pivotal role in advancing the strategic and academic objectives of the Prince Claus Chair. The research focuses on the intersection of traditional governance, post-slavery legacies, and inclusive development. By placing Maroon communities of Suriname in a comparative global context, alongside other traditional societies and African-descended populations, the PCC aims to deepen our understanding of shared governance challenges and strategies. The research by the PCC fits into current societal debates around ethnicity, land rights, sustainable development, female leadership and the social, economic and environmental impacts of utilizing natural resources. It provides an opportunity to explore important themes such as equity, access to resources, and the participation of marginalized populations. This will come together under the heading of Inclusive Governance. Research will have academic and societal impact, and tasks include: Designing and conducting research approaches and methodologies aimed at understanding Inclusive Governance, in Suriname and elsewhere; Involvement in developing research proposals, instrumentation, design, sampling, data collection, data analysis and report writing; Publishing and disseminating research findings to (inter)national academic, public and practical audiences, in multiple (written and oral) ways; Editorial roles related to writing and publishing a book on public governance and administration in Suriname. Approximately half of the available time is reserved for research aligned with the PCC’s goals, and half of the time for organizational and project support. The postdoctoral researcher will be the first point of contact for these organizational activities and will work closely with the chair holder, prof. Ine Apapoe, and representatives of the strategic theme Institutions for Open Societies, Utrecht School of Governance, REBO faculty, and other key stakeholders to ensure the successful implementation of the chair’s objectives. Key organizational responsibilities will include: Facilitating international collaboration, for example by organizing events; Developing and implementing activities within UU (IOS and REBO/USG) in line with the workplan of the PCC; Managing the visits of the PCC to Utrecht, and organizing events, workshops and meetings; Practical support to the PCC related to proposal writing, book publishing and other activities as defined in the workplan. Your qualities The researcher will be expected to demonstrate strong social and organizational skills, extensive methodological expertise, cultural awareness, the ability to work collaboratively in an international academic environment, and a commitment to advancing (interdisciplinary) research and practice. Knowledge on and/or previous working experience in Suriname is an asset. Successful candidates have the following qualifications: Ongoing or completed PhD in a relevant field; Written and oral fluency in Dutch and English; Expertise in both qualitative and quantitative research methodologies; A keen interest in governance, institutions, equity and development, and global perspectives; Experience with interdisciplinary research projects; Strong social and organizational skills, including project management and event coordination; A proactive attitude, with a hands-on but (culturally) sensitive work approach; Our offer a job for two years; a working week of 28 - 40 (0,8-1 FTE) hours and a gross monthly salary between €3.706 and €5.760 (salary scale 10 under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)). The salary is based on a 38-hour working week; 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment set out in our collective labour agreement, we offer attractive additional benefits, including opportunities for personal and professional growth , flexible leave arrangements, and extra vacation days. Through the UU Terms of Employment Options Model, you can tailor your employment package to your needs. In this way, we encourage you to grow in what you do, both in your work and in your development. Read more about our terms of employment . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . The Faculty of Law, Economics and Governance is a faculty at the heart of society, with a strong focus on social issues. Our subjects of Law, Economics and Governance give us a strong mix of academic disciplines that complement and enhance each other. This is a unique combination and a mark of our faculty's strength. Utrecht University School of Governance (USG) is one of the three departments of the Faculty of Law, Economics and Governance of Utrecht University. USG studies public issues and public organisations in their interaction with the developments in politics and society. IOS Institutions for Open Societies (IOS) , one of the strategic themes of the UU, brings together scholars from different disciplines working on key topics that are essential for building or maintaining sustainable, open and resilient societies. Prins Claus Chair The Prince Claus Chair in Equity and Development was established by Utrecht University and the International Institute of Social Studies of Erasmus University Rotterdam in early 2003 in honor of his royal highness Prince Claus of the Netherlands (1926-2002). Chairholder Ine Apapoe hopes to change the way societies make youth policy: ‘\"An appointment at the PCC represents more than a personal milestone; it is an opportunity to make a significant contribution to my research ambitions and professional development, while also offering the chance to strengthen the Anton de Kom University in the scientific realm\" says Ine Apapoe. \"I view my work as a bridge between academic research and community knowledge, grounded in both empirical data and cultural insights. Through this approach, I aim to contribute to a more inclusive and equitable scientific framework, ensuring that the voices and experiences of the Maroon community in Suriname are recognized and heard in discussions regarding governance, decision-making processes, development, and sustainability—both within Suriname and beyond.\" This exciting job opportunity combines a challenging research component and organizational component both of which are critical in making the ambitions of this Prince Claus Chair possible. More information For more information, please contact Marnix de Witte at m.a.dewitte@uu.nl Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Bijlhouwerstraat 6-8 , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page . Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. To apply, please send your curriculum vitae, including a letter of motivation, via the ‘apply now’ button. The application deadline is 1 November 2026. Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/postdoc-in-developmental-psychology": {
      "title": "Postdoc in Developmental Psychology",
      "description": "Postdoc in Developmental Psychology - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs Postdoc in Developmental Psychology Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Close submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Close submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests Postdoc in Developmental Psychology Faculty: Faculty of Social and Behavioural Sciences Department: Psychology Hours per week: 28 to 40 Application deadline: 1 November 2026 This 2.5-year postdoc project, funded by an NWO Open Competition Large grant, will be conducted in the Developmental Psychology Program at Utrecht University. Your job Today’s young people are coming of age in a dangerously warming world, facing uncertainty about their future. How can we address climate change in our schools? The aim of this project is to develop a transformative approach to climate change education and communication for youth. This approach integrates realistic climate information with hope and agency, moving away from traditional threat-based (“doom-and-gloom”) climate education. In particular, our approach invites youth to imagine desirable futures and translate their visions for the future into concrete actions they can take in their everyday lives. Using a mixed-method approach, we crowdsource and co-create scalable climate education and communication methods and evaluate their effects in educational settings. In this position you will be responsible for co-designing, preparing, and coordinating research, writing peer-reviewed research publications, and disseminating knowledge to inform educational and communication professionals. You will work as part of the transdisciplinary FUTUREPROOF team, bringing together expertise from psychology, education, and communication science, as well as from educational professionals and youth. You must enjoy the challenges that come with working in an emerging area of research and be comfortable with working in teams. You will receive mentorship in further developing their own, independent program of research and grant development. Your qualities We are looking for a highly motivated candidate who is interested in research at the intersection of psychology, education and communication science. You enjoy working in a collaborative environment in which you have the freedom to work independently, while also contributing to a shared goal. You take a mastery-oriented approach to your own learning and development and are eager to support the learning and development of more junior researchers. Experience in climate education and communication is not essential, but would be an advantage. Requirements: You hold a PhD in psychology, education, communication, prevention science or a related discipline. You have a proven ability to address original and innovative research questions using rigorous research methods. Experience with mixed-methods research, for example integrating participatory, co-creative and experimental methods, would be an advantage. You take a proactive, collaborative and creative approach to your work. You are flexible and have strong organisational skills. You enjoy working as part of a team while also being able to work independently and take ownership of your work. You have excellent writing and presentation skills in English; proficiency in Dutch is preferred. As a postdoc, you will be part of the transdisciplinary FUTUREPROOF team, bringing together expertise from psychology, education and communication science, as well as from educational professionals and young people. You enjoy the challenges of working in an emerging area of research and feel comfortable working collaboratively in a multidisciplinary team. You will receive mentorship to further develop your own independent research programme and build your experience in grant development. Our offer a job for 1 year, with possibility to extend for an additional 18 months; a working week of 28 - 40 hours and a gross monthly salary between €4.251 and €4.925 (salary mid scale 10, bottom scale 11 under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)). The salary is based on a 38-hour working week; 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment set out in our collective labour agreement, we offer attractive additional benefits, including opportunities for personal and professional growth , flexible leave arrangements, and extra vacation days. Through the UU Terms of Employment Options Model, you can tailor your employment package to your needs. In this way, we encourage you to grow in what you do, both in your work and in your development. Read more about our terms of employment . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . The Faculty of Social and Behavioural Sciences is one of the leading faculties in Europe providing research and academic teaching in cultural anthropology, educational sciences, interdisciplinary social science, pedagogical sciences, psychology, and sociology. Almost 7,000 students are enrolled in a broad range of undergraduate and graduate programmes. The Faculty of Social and Behavioural Sciences has some 1,100 faculty and staff members, all providing their individual contribution to the training and education of young talent and to the research into and finding solutions for scientific and societal issues. The faculty is located at Utrecht Science Park near the historical city centre of Utrecht. At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Martinus J. Langeveldgebouw , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page . More information For more information, please contact Prof. Sander Thomaes at s.thomaes@uu.nl Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now Applications should include a (1) motivation letter, (2) full CV, and (3) copy of a publication of which you are especially proud. If you are invited for an interview, you will be asked to provide two letters of recommendation, preferably of a (former) supervisor or other academic collaborator. Send your application via the ‘apply now’ button. The first round of interviews takes place in November 2026. Our preferred start date is January 1, 2027. Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. Please note that international candidates that need a visa/work permit for the Netherlands require at least four months processing time after selection and acceptance. Our International Service Desk (ISD) can answer your questions about living in the Netherlands as international staff . Finding appropriate housing in or near Utrecht is your own responsibility, but the ISD may be able to advise you therewith. In case of general questions about working and living in The Netherlands, please consult the Dutch Mobility Portal. The application deadline is 1 November 2026. Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/two-locum-positions-in-equine-reproduction-for-5-months-at-utrecht-university": {
      "title": "Two locum positions in Equine Reproduction for 5 months",
      "description": "Two locum positions in Equine Reproduction for 5 months at Utrecht University - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs Two locum positions in Equine Reproduction for 5 months at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Open submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Open submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests Two locum positions in Equine Reproduction for 5 months at Utrecht University Faculty: Faculty of Veterinary Medicine Department: Department Clinical Sciences Hours per week: 36 to 40 Application deadline: 30 October 2026 Apply now The successful candidate(s) (locum) will join a ‘reproduction team’ consisting of five veterinarians (one ACT diplomate, three ECAR diplomats, and one intern), three clinical assistants, three lab assistants, a secretary and a group of dedicated researchers. We are responsible for examining and/or treating a wide range of reproductive conditions in mares and stallions. Your job In particular, we offer a broad range of assisted reproductive technologies in mares including embryo transfer, and ovum-pick up as clinical services. During the breeding season, we inseminate approximately 300 mares per season, perform around 150 embryo flushes and transfer approximately 850 embryos (flushed and frozen-thawed ICSI embryos). The appointee will be involved in clinical duties (routine reproductive management of donor and recipient mares, monitoring and breeding management of mares inseminated with frozen semen, embryo flushes,…) and associated teaching (students and intern training). The appointee will also participate in out-of-hours duties (routine weekend work (1/3); obstetrical emergencies).ty. Your qualities You are a veterinarian with extensive experience of clinical equine reproduction including assisted reproductive technologies and preferably, although not a requirement, a post-graduate diploma in equine reproduction (i.e. European College of Animal Reproduction or American College of Theriogenologists). The University Equine Clinic aspires to quality, efficiency and a high standard of customer service; you share this vision, are a good team-worker and an able communicator. You are registered in the Dutch Register of Veterinary Medical Practitioners (Diergeneeskunderegister) and are legally authorised to practise your profession in the Netherlands. If you obtained your diploma abroad, you must complete the applicable recognition and registration procedure. For more information about the requirements and registration process, please visit the Information in English | Diergeneeskunderegister Our offer a job for full-time position for a period of 5 months a working week of 36 - 40 hours and a gross monthly salary between €3.706 and €5.760 (salary scale 10, 11 under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)). The salary is based on a 38-hour working week; 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment set out in our collective labour agreement, we offer attractive additional benefits, including opportunities for personal and professional growth , flexible leave arrangements, and extra vacation days. Through the UU Terms of Employment Options Model, you can tailor your employment package to your needs. In this way, we encourage you to grow in what you do, both in your work and in your development. Read more about our terms of employment . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . At the Faculty of Veterinary Medicine we train the veterinarians and researchers of the future, provide care for animals and conduct leading and societal relevant research. That is what we are good at. We see that the health and welfare of animals, humans and the environment are interconnected. By sharing our knowledge and working together, we make positive impact, both nationally and internationally. Our 1,500 students and 950 staff members inspire and strengthen each other. Our engagement connects us. The drive to, from an integral perspective, make the world a better place. Clinical Sciences At the Department of Clinical Sciences , you will work alongside approximately 150 veterinarians and a similar number of support staff. Approximately two-thirds of the veterinarians are specialists in various disciplines; the remaining one-third consists of interns, residents and PhD students. Together we are responsible for the education of veterinary students, provision of clinical services to horses and companion animals, and both clinical and more fundamental research in the areas of regenerative medicine, locomotion, genetics and reproduction. More information For further details and informal inquiries, please contact Anthony Claes or Bart Leemans by e-mail a.claes@uu.nl or b.leemans@uu.nl (or phone +31 (0)30 253 1188). Please do NOT send your application to this email address. Please send your application accompanied by a letter of motivation and a curriculum vitae. You can apply via the 'application' button below. Application deadline: December 1st 2026. Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion external link . If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Willem C. Schimmelgebouw external link , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page external link . A valid Certificate of Conduct (VOG) is required for this position. This will be requested as part of the application process. Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. To apply, please send your curriculum vitae, including a letter of motivation, via the ‘apply now’ button. The application deadline is 30 October 2026. Apply now Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/phd-reconstructing-zeelands-historic-landscape-developments": {
      "title": "PhD: Reconstructing Zeeland’s historic landscape developments",
      "description": "PhD: Reconstructing Zeeland’s historic landscape developments - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs PhD: Reconstructing Zeeland’s historic landscape developments Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Open submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Open submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests PhD: Reconstructing Zeeland’s historic landscape developments Faculty: Faculty of Geosciences Department: Department of Physical Geography Hours per week: 36 to 40 Application deadline: 1 November 2026 Apply now The PhD position is part of the larger research project Re-Flex Zeeland that aims to reconstruct and visualize Zeeland's natural landscape formation processes during historical periods of rapid sea-level rise, with a focus on the last 12,000 years. The project is funded by the Delta Climate Centre (DCC) in Vlissingen and consists of two PhDs and one postdoc position in a collaboration between Utrecht University, Wageningen University & Research (WUR), NIOZ, Scalda and UNESCO GeoPark Schelde Delta. Your job The present-day landscape of Zeeland is highly regulated to support key societal, economic, and flood-safety functions. Allowing more space for natural processes to shape the landscape can contribute to flood protection and restoration of biodiversity, however, knowledge of how natural landscapes have evolved in the past and of the ecosystem services they once provided remains limited. This phenomenon is often described as shifting baselines, whereby each generation perceives the contemporary environment as the long-term norm. In reality, the Dutch landscape—and that of Zeeland in particular—has been extraordinarily dynamic and adjusting flexibly to changes in sea level. Over the Holocene, Zeeland has transitioned from an subarctic environment with boreal forest to alternating phases of peatland and intertidal environments, before becoming largely regulated through human embankment only in the past few centuries. For the work package “Holocene peat deposits as natural archives of landscape developments” we look for a motivated PhD student who investigates these landscape and ecosystem dynamics in Zeeland based on fossil plant remains preserved in peat and lake sediment records. What will you be doing? You will design and carry out field work to map the locations and ages of peat deposits along an east-west transect around the major marine estuaries of Zeeland. You will integrate existing and new field data and investigate the botanical composition of the peat and, using pollen analysis, improve the current knowledge on past landscape reconstructions with a focus on peat to intertidal transitions. You will also co-supervise BSc and MSc courses and thesis projects that contribute to this. The gathered data will provide new insight in the exact timing and variability in past landscape changes. Your results will furthermore contribute to computer simulations of coastal dynamics in a parallel PhD project conducted at WUR and provide context for visualisations of historic landscapes developed by a postdoc at NIOZ in a collaboration with Scalda and UNESCO GeoPark Schelde Delta. Your qualities We are looking for an enthusiastic PhD candidate who is interested in landscape and vegetation development through time across different timescales and a drive to understand the relevant processes involved. The project physical geographical field work, pollen analysis and quantitative vegetation reconstruction. We require at least significant experience in one of these aspects and a motivation to develop skills in the other. The project will involve several sampling campaigns in Zeeland, in collaboration with the DCC, and training courses for the relevant techniques. You have a broad interest in geosciences, are a team player who is able to work in interdisciplinary environment, and are curiosity-driven. We are looking for a candidate: with an MSc degree in Physical Geography, Earth Sciences, Landscape Ecology, or a related field; who has a drive to link and collaborate with different geoscientific disciplines; with demonstrated experience with paleogeographic and/or botanical landscape reconstructions; with affinity with Zeeland and willingness to spend time in and engage with the Zeeland landscape and community; with good English oral and writing skills: English at C1 level or better (comparable to level 3 of the Lecturer Assessment Grid) qualifies, or willingness and aptitude to obtain this level by training. PhD candidates at Utrecht University follow courses as part of their own academic and personal development. They also assist in teaching courses at Bachelor's and Master's level at our faculty. Both activities amount to approximately 10% of the contracted time. Our offer We offer : a position (1.0 FTE) for 1 year, with an extension to a total of four years upon a successful assessment in the first year, and with the specific intent that it results in a doctorate within this period; a working week of 36 - 40 hours and a gross monthly salary between €3.204 and €4.051 (salary scale P under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)). The salary is based on a 38-hour working week; 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment set out in our collective labour agreement, we offer attractive additional benefits, including opportunities for personal and professional growth , flexible leave arrangements, and extra vacation days. Through the UU Terms of Employment Options Model, you can tailor your employment package to your needs. In this way, we encourage you to grow in what you do, both in your work and in your development. Read more about our terms of employment . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . Utrecht University’s Faculty of Geosciences studies the Earth: from the Earth’s core to its surface, including man’s spatial and material utilisation of the Earth – always with a focus on sustainability and innovation. With 3,400 students (BSc and MSc) and 720 staff, the faculty is a strong and challenging organisation. The Faculty of Geosciences is organised in four Departments: Earth Sciences, Human Geography & Spatial Planning, Physical Geography, and Sustainable Development. The city of Utrecht is one of the oldest cities in the Netherlands, with a charming old centre and an internationally oriented culture that is strongly influenced by its century-old university. Utrecht has been consistently ranked as one of the most livable cities in the Netherlands. The team of the Department of Physical Geography excels in research and education on BSc, MSc and PhD level. We research processes, patterns and dynamics of Earth’s continental and coastal systems, and the interaction in between. This knowledge is essential for the sustainable management of our planet and to guarantee the availability of resources for the next generations. We are a lively department that hosts an active PhD candidate community of more than thirty PhD students. Will you join our team? More information For further information about the vacancy, please contact: Dr T.H. Donders external link (Associate Professor) via t.h.donders@uu.nl or Dr W.Z. Hoek external link (Associate Professor) via w.z.hoek@uu.nl Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. external link If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Vening Meineszgebouw A external link , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page external link . Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. You can only apply via our online procedure using the 'apply now' button. E-mail applications cannot be accepted. Please include the following information in your application: a letter of motivation your curriculum vitae the names of at least 2 referees (with an email contact addresses and phone numbers. We look forward to receive your application no later than November 1st, 2026. The first interviews will be scheduled mid November. The intended starting date for the PhD project is late 2026 or early 2027. If you have an international (non-Dutch) Master’s diploma, you will be requested to provide your Bachelor’s and Master’s diplomas as well as the corresponding grade lists (in English) if you are selected for this position. Note that international candidates that need a visa/work permit for the Netherlands require at least four months processing time after selection and acceptance. This will be arranged with help of the International Service Desk (ISD) of our university. Finding appropriate housing in or near Utrecht is your own responsibility, but the ISD may be able to advise you therewith. Unfortunately, we must warn that it is a tight market at the moment. In case of general questions about working and living in The Netherlands, please consult the Dutch Mobility Portal . The application deadline is 1 November 2026. Apply now Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/postdoc-multi-risk-adaptation-pathways-for-river-delta-systems": {
      "title": "Postdoc: multi-risk adaptation pathways for river delta systems",
      "description": "Postdoc: multi-risk adaptation pathways for river delta systems - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs Postdoc: multi-risk adaptation pathways for river delta systems Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Close submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Close submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests Postdoc: multi-risk adaptation pathways for river delta systems Faculty: Faculty of Geosciences Department: Department of Physical Geography Hours per week: 36 to 40 Application deadline: 16 November 2026 River delta regions worldwide are increasingly threatened by sea-level rise and extreme hydrological events. Water managers must adapt to these hazards while balancing competing sectoral priorities, such as maintaining clean and affordable drinking water supplies, minimizing flood damage, and preserving ecosystems. Your job The Dynamic Adaptive Pathways Planning for Multi-Risk (DAPP-MR) framework helps decision-makers navigate these challenges by identifying low-regret near-term actions and avoiding costly decision lock-ins. Building on this framework, this position focuses on designing robust multi-risk pathways for complex multi-sectoral delta systems. Your job In this 2-year postdoc, you will explore new approaches for developing multi-risk adaptation pathways that inform robust decision-making while balancing multi-sectoral trade-offs. You will start by working with decision-makers and stakeholders in the Netherlands to characterize current and future risks in the Dutch delta and identify trade-offs between sectoral priorities. You will then develop methods for designing adaptation pathways that navigate these risks, and evaluate your approach against state-of-the-art adaptive pathway planning. You will share your results in stakeholder meetings, scientific conferences, and academic journals. Your work will help Dutch water managers plan adaptation under uncertainty and contribute approaches that can be transferred to deltas worldwide. Your qualities We are looking for an enthusiastic and collaborative postdoctoral researcher with a strong technical background and a motivation to make climate adaptation research useful to society. Must-have criteria: a PhD in Civil Engineering, Earth Science, Environmental Science, (Socio-) Hydrology, Physical Geography, or a related field; has proven knowledge and peer-reviewed publication record in one or more of the above-mentioned fields; shows a willingness to integrate insights from stakeholder engagement and participatory research with quantitative system modelling of our research group; experience with systems modeling and computer programming (e.g. Python); strong communication and scientific writing skills in English. Helpful criteria: experience with Dynamic Adaptive Pathways Planning or other methods from Decision Making Under Deep Uncertainty; experience using Multi-objective Optimization; experience working with stakeholders on climate adaptation decision-making; experience in river, coastal, or delta systems modelling; interest in highly interdisciplinary collaborations. Our offer We offer: a position ( 1.0 FTE) for 24-36 months. a working week of 36 - 40 hours and a gross monthly salary between €3.706 and €5.760 (salary scale 10 under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)). The salary is based on a 38-hour working week; 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment set out in our collective labour agreement, we offer attractive additional benefits, including opportunities for personal and professional growth , flexible leave arrangements, and extra vacation days. Through the UU Terms of Employment Options Model, you can tailor your employment package to your needs. In this way, we encourage you to grow in what you do, both in your work and in your development. Read more about our terms of employment . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . Utrecht University’s Faculty of Geosciences studies the Earth: from the Earth’s core to its surface, including man’s spatial and material utilisation of the Earth – always with a focus on sustainability and innovation. With 3,400 students (BSc and MSc) and 720 staff, the faculty is a strong and challenging organisation. The Faculty of Geosciences is organised in four Departments: Earth Sciences, Human Geography & Spatial Planning, Physical Geography, and Sustainable Development. More information For more information about this position, please contact David Gold at d.f.gold@uu.nl Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Vening Meineszgebouw A , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page . Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. To apply, please send your curriculum vitae, including a letter of motivation, via the ‘apply now’ button. The first round of interviews will take place on the week of 30 November, 2026. If you have an international (non-Dutch) Master’s diploma, you will be requested to provide your Bachelor’s and Master’s diplomas as well as the corresponding grade lists (in English) if you are selected for this position. Note that international candidates that need a visa/work permit for the Netherlands require at least four months processing time after selection and acceptance. This will be arranged with help of the International Service Desk (ISD) of our university. Finding appropriate housing in or near Utrecht is your own responsibility, but the ISD may be able to advise you therewith. Unfortunately, we must warn that it is a tight market at the moment. In case of general questions about working and living in The Netherlands, please consult the Dutch Mobility Portal . The application deadline is 16 November 2026. Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    },
    "https://www.uu.nl/en/organisation/working-at-utrecht-university/jobs/postdoc-responsible-scaling-of-digital-innovation-in-mental-healthcare": {
      "title": "Postdoc: Responsible scaling of digital innovation in mental healthcare",
      "description": "Postdoc: Responsible scaling of digital innovation in mental healthcare - Working at Utrecht University - Utrecht University Skip to main content Menu Search uu.nl Search scope Subject Staff member Search Select language Nederlands nl Breadcrumb UU.nl Organisation Working at Utrecht University Jobs Postdoc: Responsible scaling of digital innovation in mental healthcare Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Working at Utrecht University Jobs Close submenu + Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Close submenu + HR excellence in research Leadership International staff and guests UU home Working at Utrecht University Jobs Participation Act Privacy statement for job applicants Contact Terms of employment Professional development Leadership International staff and guests Postdoc: Responsible scaling of digital innovation in mental healthcare Faculty: Faculty of Geosciences Department: Department of Sustainable Development Hours per week: 32 to 40 Application deadline: 8 November 2026 Are you passionate about understanding how digital innovations are scaled up in a responsible way? Do you want to contribute to better understanding how these innovations become applied in mental healthcare? Then this opportunity at the Copernicus Institute of Sustainable Development is for you. Your job During the development of many new products and services, insufficient thought is given to how these innovations could scale in a socially responsible way. We assume that innovators, together with a broad group of stakeholders, must think about important conditions for successful scaling at an early stage of the innovation process. At the moment, there is a need for a better understanding how to approach responsible scaling. In the project you will learn about how innovations can improve local day-to-day practice in mental healthcare, and about the systemic conditions to facilitate this local implementation, such as regulations, participation, governance, funding, etc. For this, a new theory for responsible scaling needs to be developed, resulting in diffusion pathways allow for contextualization and tailored implementation. These pathways should include various societal perspectives and values (ethics, public values, company’s interests) from the very start. They should also be able to align with – or recommend changes for – institutional boundaries in the (mental) healthcare system. As a postdoctoral researcher in this project, you will conduct in-depth empirical studies on the dynamics of responsible scaling in digital mental healthcare. Broadly, the following research activities are foreseen for this project: Conceptualizing diffusion and scaling theories of innovation; Investigating scaling pathways in the context of existing cases in mental health, by looking at routines, values, assumptions of the different organizations and users involved in the development and upscaling of digital innovations; Exploring possible future scaling pathways for responsible scaling and recommendations on how to build them and under which circumstances to use them; Uncovering success and failure factors that facilitate or hinder scaling specifically in eHealth for mental health. As a postdoctoral researcher you will become embedded in a larger project on responsible scaling of digital innovation in mental healthcare , and interact with a wide range of interesting stakeholders, including technology developers, mental health organizations, patient groups, reimbursement agencies, etc. You will also be involved in exciting scientific communities on innovation and technology assessment. The project will be supervised by Dr Lotte Krabbenborg and Prof. Wouter Boon . Your qualities You are a motivated and goal-oriented researcher who would like to work in an interdisciplinary research environment. Specifically, you bring the following: You have a successfully completed PhD degree. Relevant disciplines include (but are not limited to) science and technology studies (STS), transition studies, innovation studies, information science, medical humanities, and sociology; You are interested in responsible upscaling of digital innovations; You have an affinity with in-depth social research and innovative methodologies with a mixed-methods or qualitative focus; You have an affinity and experience with engagement and communication of scientific research; You enjoy working in an interdisciplinary team, and can showcase a critical attitude, intellectual independence, and good organizational skills; You are interested in collaborating with societal partners as part the research project; You have excellent written and verbal communication skills in English. Our offer We offer: a temporary position (0.8-1.0 FTE) for 29 months; a working week of 32 - 40 hours and a gross monthly salary between €3.706 and €5.760 (salary scale 10 under the Collective Labour Agreement for Dutch Universities (CAO NU)). The salary is based on a 38-hour working week; 8% holiday pay and 8.3% year-end bonus; a pension scheme, partially paid parental leave and flexible terms of employment based on the CAO NU. In addition to the terms of employment set out in our collective labour agreement, we offer attractive additional benefits, including opportunities for personal and professional growth , flexible leave arrangements, and extra vacation days. Through the UU Terms of Employment Options Model, you can tailor your employment package to your needs. In this way, we encourage you to grow in what you do, both in your work and in your development. Read more about our terms of employment . About us A better future for everyone. This ambition motivates our scientists in executing their leading research and inspiring teaching. At Utrecht University , the various disciplines collaborate intensively towards major strategic themes . Our focus is on Dynamics of Youth, Institutions for Open Societies, Life Sciences and Pathways to Sustainability. Sharing science, shaping tomorrow . Utrecht University’s Faculty of Geosciences studies the Earth: from the Earth’s core to its surface, including man’s spatial and material utilisation of the Earth – always with a focus on sustainability and innovation. With 3,400 students (BSc and MSc) and 720 staff, the faculty is a strong and challenging organisation. The Faculty of Geosciences is organised in four Departments: Earth Sciences, Human Geography & Spatial Planning, Physical Geography, and Sustainable Development. The city of Utrecht is one of the oldest cities in the Netherlands, with a charming old centre and an internationally oriented culture that is strongly influenced by its century-old university. Utrecht has been consistently ranked as one of the most livable cities in the Netherlands. More information For more information about this position, please contact Prof. Wouter Boon at w.p.c.boon@uu.nl Candidates for this vacancy will be recruited by Utrecht University. Apply now At Utrecht University, we strive to be a place where everyone feels at home. We value colleagues with different backgrounds, perspectives and identities, including differences in culture, religion or ethnicity, gender, sexual orientation, neurodiversity, disability and age. We are committed to creating a safe and inclusive environment where everyone can thrive and contribute. Read more about our commitment to diversity and inclusion. If you have any questions about accessibility, for example regarding your workplace Vening Meineszgebouw A , the application process or your work, please contact us via our HR contact page . Knowledge security screening can be part of the selection procedures of academic staff. We do this, among other things, to prevent the unwanted transfer of sensitive knowledge and technology. To apply, please send us the following documents via the 'apply now' button: Your curriculum vitae (CV), including appendixes with grade transcripts for your Bachelor’s and Master’s programme, and the names and contact details of 2-3 references; A motivation letter of no more than two A4 pages explaining your interest and suitability for the position. The application deadline is 8 November 2026. Share Share on LinkedIn Share on Facebook Share on WhatsApp Share via email Footer menu (EN) Go to Choose a programme Student information Jobs Alumni Giving Services Intranet for employees Course planner My Timetable About UU Contact Directory Disclaimer Privacy Contact Follow UU Facebook Youtube LinkedIn Instagram Utrecht University Heidelberglaan 8 3584 CS Utrecht The Netherlands Tel. +31 (0)30 253 35 50"
    }
  },
